B.EM=new A.b4("Variation","Breaks each block into the photo\u2019s own patches of color, keeping its value. Shows the broken color you can lay into a mass once the big shapes are right.","V")
B.EN=new A.b4("Reset","Back to the splits that come from the photo itself.",null)
B.EO=new A.b4("Export the palette","Saves the colors as a spreadsheet (CSV), with their codes and values, for your notes or for planning mixtures.",null)
B.EP=new A.b4("Hide the side panel",u.W,"P")
B.EQ=new A.b4("More detail","One level further: the shapes break into smaller planes.","]")
B.ER=new A.b4("Notan","Just two values, light and dark. Checks the design: light and shadow should read as two clear, simple shapes before anything else.","3")
B.ES=new A.b4("Clean","Each block gets one clear color, a steady value with its saturation kept, never a muddy average: a good first mixture for each shape.",null)
B.ET=new A.b4("Style","How the block-in is made: edges, brush, broken color, and how each block\u2019s color is chosen.",null)
B.EU=new A.b4("Hold to see the photo","Shows the photo while you hold it, then the block-in again: flick between the two to judge shapes and values against the reference.","Space")
B.EV=new A.b4("Magnifier","How much the eyedropper\u2019s loupe enlarges what is under it.",null)
B.EW=new A.b4("Study","Where the values, notan or temperature study splits, and into how many groups. Choose a study view first.",null)
B.EX=new A.b4("Show markers",u.z,null)
B.EY=new A.b4("Grid","Turn the drawing grid on and set its cells, to transfer the drawing.",null)
B.EZ=new A.b4("Remove all colors","Empties this picture\u2019s palette.",null)
B.F_=new A.b4("Organic","Blocks that follow the photo\u2019s own contours: the shapes as they are, for a looser, more natural block-in.",null)
B.F0=new A.b4("Value groups","How many values the picture is grouped into. Start with three (light, middle, dark) as in a value sketch, and add more as the painting develops.",null)
B.F1=new A.b4("Show numbers",u.H,null)
B.F2=new A.b4("Palette","The colors you picked, with their codes and values, and the eyedropper\u2019s settings.",null)
B.n8=new A.b4("Open a picture","Choose the reference photo to study. A photo with clear light and shadow gives the strongest block-in.","Ctrl+O")
B.n9=new A.b4("Drawing grid","Squares up the picture as in the grid method: draw the same grid on your canvas to place shapes and proportions accurately.","G")
B.F3=new A.b4("Line color","Light lines show up on a dark picture, dark lines on a light one.",null)
B.F4=new A.b4("Dominant","Each block takes the color that covers most of it: closer to how the area reads at a glance, though it can lean grayer.",null)
B.F5=new A.b4("Tolerance","How close a color must be to count as the same. Raise it to include near matches, lower it for this exact color.",null)
B.F6=new A.b4("Values","Lightness only, in three to six groups, like squinting at the subject. Get the value pattern right first: a painting reads by its values more than by its colors.","2")
B.na=new A.b4("About Colorblocking","The version, the licenses, and how your pictures stay private.","F1")
B.nb=new A.b4("Detail","How far the block-in is taken: from the three or four biggest masses at the left to a refined block-in at the right. Each level divides the one before, like the stages of a painting: start with the big shapes, then refine.","[ and ]")
B.F7=new A.b4("Remove","Takes this color out of the palette.","Delete")
B.F8=new A.b4("Square cells","Makes every cell square, as on graph paper. Off, the picture is divided into equal rows and columns instead.",null)
B.F9=new A.b4("HSL","Hue (the color\u2019s place on the color wheel, in degrees), saturation and lightness. Tap to copy.",null)
B.Fa=new A.b4("Subdivide","Divides each cell again with fainter lines, for passages that need more care, like eyes or hands.",null)
B.Fb=new A.b4("Invert","Swaps light and dark, to see the dark shapes as the design instead.",null)
B.Fc=new A.b4("Color","The block-in, or the photo, in full color.","1")
B.Fd=new A.b4("LAB","CIELAB: L* is lightness from 0 to 100, a* runs from green to red, b* from blue to yellow. Tap to copy.",null)
B.Fe=new A.b4("Bigger masses","One level simpler: fewer, larger shapes.","[")
B.Ff=new A.b4("Brush","Lets soft edges break up into strokes that follow the form, the way a brush loses an edge, while hard, contrasting edges stay crisp. Shows where you can soften and lose edges in paint.","S")
B.Fg=new A.b4("Highlight these areas",u.C,null)
B.Fh=new A.b4("Splits","The photo\u2019s values or temperatures from one end to the other, with a line where the groups divide. Drag a line to decide which shapes belong to the light and which to the shadow.",null)
B.Fi=new A.b4("Temperature","Warm against cool, relative to this picture. Painters push these shifts, warm light against cool shadow or the other way round, to make the light feel real.","4")
B.Fj=new A.b4("Value","Lightness on the painter\u2019s scale from 0 (black) to 10 (white), from CIELAB L*: compare it with a value scale or your swatches.",null)
B.Fk=new A.b4("Angular","Blocks with straight edges, like the planes of an academic or Sargent-style block-in. Helps you see form as a few flat planes turning from light into shadow.",null)
B.Fl=new A.b4("Lines","The outlines of the block-in on paper, or over the photo with Original: a drawing of the big shapes to transfer to your canvas.","5")
B.Fm=new A.b4("Sample size","How many pixels the eyedropper averages in the photo. One pixel gives the exact pixel; larger sizes smooth out grain and texture, closer to the color you would mix.",null)
B.nc=new A.b4("Close","Hides this message, or stops the eyedropper or a highlight.","Esc")
